<div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;margin-bottom:16px"><span style="padding:2px 10px;border-radius:4px;background:rgba(244,114,182,.15);color:#f9a8d4;font-size:.7em;font-weight:700">🔬 Scientist</span></div>
<h2>Symplectic Geometry — The Geometry of Hamiltonian Mechanics</h2>
<p style="font-size:.95em;color:var(--text-sec);margin-bottom:16px">Symplectic geometry is the study of symplectic manifolds — even-dimensional smooth manifolds with a closed, non-degenerate 2-form ω. It's the natural mathematical framework for Hamiltonian mechanics: the phase space of a classical system is a symplectic manifold. The field includes Hamiltonian dynamics, moment maps, symplectic reduction (Marsden-Weinstein), Floer homology, Gromov-Witten invariants, and mirror symmetry.</p>
<div class="quote-box"><div class="quote">"Symplectic geometry is the geometry of phase space — where the laws of classical mechanics unfold."</div><div class="attribution">— Vladimir Arnold (paraphrased)</div></div>
<div class="section-label part1">Part 1</div>
<h2>🌀 Symplectic Manifolds and Darboux's Theorem</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Phase Space</div><div class="story-title">Symplectic Forms, Lagrangian Submanifolds, and Darboux Coordinates</div><div class="story-text">A <strong>symplectic manifold</strong> (M,ω) has ω closed (dω=0) and non-degenerate (ω∧n ≠ 0). The standard example is ℝ^{2n} with ω₀ = Σ dp_i∧dq_i. <strong>Darboux's theorem</strong>: every symplectic manifold is locally symplectomorphic to (ℝ^{2n}, ω₀) — there are no local invariants, unlike Riemannian geometry. The Hamiltonian vector field X_H is defined by ω(X_H, ·) = dH — its flow preserves ω. The <strong>Poisson bracket</strong> {F,G} = ω(X_F, X_G) makes C^∞(M) into a Lie algebra. A <strong>Lagrangian submanifold</strong> L ⊂ M is a maximal isotropic submanifold (dim n, ω|_L = 0). The graph of a symplectomorphism is Lagrangian in (M×M, ω⊕−ω). The <strong>Weinstein tubular neighborhood theorem</strong>: a Lagrangian has a neighborhood symplectomorphic to T*L.</div><div class="story-moral">Darboux's theorem says all symplectic manifolds look the same locally — the global geometry is captured by topology and the choice of compatible almost complex structure.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part2">Part 2</div>
<h2>⚖️ Moment Maps and Symplectic Reduction</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Reduction</div><div class="story-title">Moment Maps, Marsden-Weinstein Reduction, and Toric Varieties</div><div class="story-text">A <strong>Hamiltonian group action</strong> of G on M has a <strong>moment map</strong> μ: M → g^* satisfying d⟨μ,ξ⟩ = ω(ξ_M, ·) where ξ_M is the fundamental vector field. For G = S¹ acting on (ℂⁿ, ω₀ = i/2 Σ dz_k∧dz̄_k), μ(z) = Σ |z_k|². The <strong>Marsden-Weinstein quotient</strong> (symplectic reduction): M//G = μ^{-1}(0)/G is a symplectic manifold (if 0 is a regular value and the action is free). This is how one builds new symplectic manifolds from old ones — e.g., ℙⁿ = ℂ^{n+1}//S¹. <strong>Toric varieties</strong> are symplectic quotients of ℂ^N by a torus — their moment polytopes (Delzant polytopes) classify them. The <strong>Atiyah-Guillemin-Sternberg theorem</strong>: the image of the moment map of a torus action is a convex polytope — the convex hull of the images of fixed points.</div><div class="story-moral">Symplectic reduction is the geometric version of eliminating symmetries in mechanics — the quotient of the zero level set of the moment map is a new symplectic manifold.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part3">Part 3</div>
<h2>🌸 Floer Homology and Pseudoholomorphic Curves</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Holomorphic</div><div class="story-title">Gromov's Compactness, Floer Homology, and Arnold's Conjecture</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Gromov's theorem</strong> (1985): pseudoholomorphic curves in a symplectic manifold with a compatible almost complex structure satisfy a compactness theorem — sequences converge to nodal curves (bubbling). This is the foundation of modern symplectic topology. <strong>Floer homology</strong> (Floer, 1988) counts pseudoholomorphic cylinders connecting two Lagrangian submanifolds — it's an invariant of the pair. <strong>Hamiltonian Floer homology</strong> studies fixed points of Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms and proved the <strong>Arnold conjecture</strong>: the number of fixed points of a Hamiltonian diffeomorphism is at least the rank of H*(M). The <strong>PSS isomorphism</strong> (Piunikhin-Salamon-Schwarz) relates Floer homology to quantum cohomology. <strong>Gromov-Witten invariants</strong> count pseudoholomorphic curves in M with constraints — they define the <strong>quantum cup product</strong>, making H*(M) into a Frobenius manifold. <strong>Kontsevich's homological mirror symmetry</strong>: the Fukaya category (Lagrangian Floer theory) of M is equivalent to the derived category of coherent sheaves of its mirror.</div><div class="story-moral">Pseudoholomorphic curves are the central tool of modern symplectic geometry — they give invariants (Floer homology, GW invariants) that detect deep structure.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part4">Part 4</div>
<h2>🪞 Mirror Symmetry and Modern Directions</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Duality</div><div class="story-title">Mirror Symmetry, Fukaya Categories, and Spectral Networks</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Mirror symmetry</strong> (Candelas-de la Ossa-Green-Parkes, 1991): Calabi-Yau 3-folds come in mirror pairs (M, W) with Hodge diamonds mirrored: h^{p,q}(M) = h^{3−p,q}(W). The A-model (symplectic, counting holomorphic curves) on M equals the B-model (complex, algebraic deformations) on W. The <strong>Fukaya category</strong> Fuk(M) has Lagrangian submanifolds as objects and Floer cohomology as morphisms. The <strong>derived category</strong> D^b Coh(W) has coherent sheaves as objects. Kontsevich's HMS conjecture: Fuk(M) ≅ D^b Coh(W). <strong>Stability conditions</strong> (Bridgeland) on derived categories give a wall-and-chamber decomposition — they're related to the space of complexified Kähler classes. <strong>Spectral networks</strong> (Gaiotto-Moore-Neitzke) describe BPS states in 4D N=2 QFTs — they're graphs on the Coulomb branch where flat sections of Hitchin's equations have jumps. <strong>Symplectic field theory</strong> (Eliashberg-Givental-Hofer) studies pseudoholomorphic curves in symplectic cobordisms, giving relative invariants.</div><div class="story-moral">Mirror symmetry reveals that symplectic geometry (counting curves) and complex geometry (studying sheaves) are two sides of the same coin — a profound duality.</div></div>
